[[:db/retract 17592186121331 :answer-field/choices 17592186121333] [:db/retract 17592186121331 :answer-field/choices 17592186121334] [:db/retract 17592186121331 :answer-field/choices 17592186121336] {:db/id 17592186124630 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-937a6bb1-f955-5ee0-bbd6-d97686bceb6d" "question-33a20d40-e8ca-5cc3-bf01-9f700d36b547" "question-71223fcf-7212-5023-82ea-b55cb18fa0b2"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124434 :question/problem "![](images/3e/3e58a5f09c9060932ee854feeec0733dc9432c2de658308e3ebdccdd0ef89e5f.svg)\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. What should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n \\!\\times\\! n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n![](images/21/21455a41af5743da6f9f4de5e267c17c6037b929a01ba894cfd91530dbb39986.svg)\n\nThe missing expression is $\\textrm{dim(Null}(A)).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124511 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a0f098cb-e297-54fe-a435-e8a16d43aa06" "question-9fbccd10-1c43-51a4-ad8b-36063e11427d"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124320 :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, and \n![](images/6a/6a294de4a5f2ff5755d8ceb5b91bb8920685a1cf2b5e727c5b5fdcfaaf0b89b0.svg)\n\nI. $A$ is not invertible\n\nII. $\\textrm{Col}(A)=\\mathbb{R}^4$\n\nIII. $\\textrm{rank}(A)=4$" :question/worked-solution "Since $A$ is a $4 \\times 4$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following: \n\n![](images/4b/4b42fd4ec8ccf491a1244a648af6fe40ce846fad388685e36d499783a095f8f7.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since \n![](images/32/327a582d15b82ebccda48358062310e6ad5c3bb2689f2024d76a8f8e7e1401a0.svg)\nwe conclude that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{dim(Null}(A))=1 \\neq 0.\n$$\n\nSo,  according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ is not invertible.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since the matrix $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{Col}(A) \\neq \\mathbb{R}^4.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $A$ is not invertible, then by the invertible matrix theorem, we must have $\\textrm{rank}(A) \\neq 4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124629 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-251cfe7e-474d-59b2-8ccf-101401daf068"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124433 :question/problem "Consider the following sentence:\n\n*If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\text{__________}\\,.$*\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nI. $\\det(A) = 0$\n\nII. $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n$\n\nIII. $A$ has $n$ pivot columns" :question/worked-solution "The invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible ![](images/dc/dc72aea1969143a926fd637d3360bb42116b27545a748923069f4ae732bd700f.svg)\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the options separately.\n\n- The following statement is false:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\det(A) = 0.$* $\\:{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nSince $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then according to the invertible matrix theorem, the matrix $A$ must be invertible, which means $\\det(A) \\ne 0.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $\\textrm{rank}(A) = n.$* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{rank}(A)=n.$\n\n- The following statement is true:\n\n$\\qquad$ *If $A$ is a $n\\times n$ matrix and $\\textrm{Null}(A) = \\{\\mathbf{ 0}\\},$ then $A$ has $n$ pivot columns.* $\\:{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$ \n\nIndeed, since $A$ is invertible, then according to the invertible matrix theorem, we have $\\textrm{dim(Col}(A))=n,$ which means that $A$ has exactly $n$ pivot columns.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124789 :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $\\{0\\}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121331 :answer-field/correct "answer-ad86e99c-d63c-534c-8616-6b38f0564843" :answer-field/choices ["answer-72b6d84b-4599-5ae1-87e9-228fcb3fc5f1" "answer-ad86e99c-d63c-534c-8616-6b38f0564843" "answer-c784e28a-945a-50b9-a3c3-88323f6f5bc6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72b6d84b-4599-5ae1-87e9-228fcb3fc5f1" :answer/id #uuid "72b6d84b-4599-5ae1-87e9-228fcb3fc5f1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{Col}(A)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad86e99c-d63c-534c-8616-6b38f0564843" :answer/id #uuid "ad86e99c-d63c-534c-8616-6b38f0564843" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{0\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c784e28a-945a-50b9-a3c3-88323f6f5bc6" :answer/id #uuid "c784e28a-945a-50b9-a3c3-88323f6f5bc6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{R}^{n}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-937a6bb1-f955-5ee0-bbd6-d97686bceb6d" :question/id #uuid "937a6bb1-f955-5ee0-bbd6-d97686bceb6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-142471" :question/problem "$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=\\bbox[3pt,white,border: 2px solid Gainsboro]{\\phantom{W}}=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the equivalence above, where $A$ is an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Which of the following should be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the *invertible matrix theorem* states the following: \n\n*Let $A$ be an $n\\,\\times \\,n$ matrix. Then we have the following equivalence:*\n\n$$\nA\\text{ is invertible}\\,⇔\\,\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Col}(A)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Col}(A))=n=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\  & \\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\} \\\\  & \\text{dim(Null}(A))=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the missing expression is $0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7bb630b8-37fa-5f0a-a193-e8a8a792e3e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7bb630b8-37fa-5f0a-a193-e8a8a792e3e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb630b8-37fa-5f0a-a193-e8a8a792e3e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-36811030-e48f-580b-b28d-88329ce34cf9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-36811030-e48f-580b-b28d-88329ce34cf9" "answer-9a1230e7-e926-5f64-a23e-9a6e2909fa72" "answer-ab023700-fa22-514b-bf5f-5062513b1047" "answer-5cde84ae-1075-5dc9-aebf-b837df68734b" "answer-8dce4f53-2c64-5